  "prompt": "Evaluate resolveWebhookStitch for every independent run and answer the contract decision questions. Use the supplied precomputed evidence for identity hashes and semantic fingerprints; do not compute SHA-256. Return the compact projection described below.\n\n# Identity evaluation output contract\n\nThis is a compact evaluation projection, version 0.1.0, not a replacement native API. Its vocabulary is supplied to both evaluation conditions. Case answers and native fixture names are excluded from model requests.\n\nEach input case has a neutral `label`, `base_input`, and ordered `runs`. Reconstruct each run independently by copying the base and replacing each top-level property named in `replace` wholesale. There is no recursive merge and no carry-over between runs. Run labels are local to their case. For canonicalization, `operation` selects email or phone. Other groups invoke the named native operation. `evidence` is auxiliary, not an argument to that operation: supplied identity digests and current semantic payload fingerprints avoid requiring SHA-256 computation. They do not assert a match, eligibility, or replay outcome.\n\nReturn exactly `{cases, decisions}` as compact JSON without pretty-printing. Preserve case and run order. Each case is `{label, runs}`; each run includes its `label` plus the group-specific fields below. Return all required fields, including nulls and empty arrays. A failed invocation has `error` set to the category below and all result fields empty/null as specified; there is no partial successful result.\n\nQualified keys are ordered string arrays: contact `[source_system, source_scope, contact_key]`; visitor `[source_system, source_scope, visitor_key]`; observation/touch `[source_system, source_scope, visitor_key, observation_key/touch_key]`; receipt `[source_system, source_scope, provider, submission_key]`. Preserve every component. Native arrays retain native ordering: qualified keys use the native JSON-key locale ordering; dedupe kept and suppressed rows each preserve their native chronological processing order with full-key ties. Do not sort away journey order. The outer case/run order is input order.\n## webhook-stitch-and-receipts\n\nEach run is `{label, error, receipt, replayed, status, method, visitor, new_visitor_scope, confidence, diagnostics}`. Error is null, `conflicting_key`, `conflicting_receipt_payload`, or `invalid_input`. On error, receipt is an empty array, replayed/status/method/visitor/new_visitor_scope/confidence are null, and diagnostics is empty.\n\n`receipt` is the qualified receipt tuple. `replayed` is the native boolean. Native status is `matched` or `created`, and method is `visitor_field`, `page_rule`, `recent_visitor`, or `new_visitor`. For a matched resolution, visitor is its qualified visitor tuple and new_visitor_scope is null. For a created resolution (including replay of that resolution), visitor is null and new_visitor_scope is `[source_system, source_scope]`; this intentionally omits the deterministic fallback digest. Confidence is the native configured number. Each diagnostic is `{code, candidates}`; missing native candidates becomes an empty list, otherwise preserve the native ordered qualified visitor keys. Codes are `visitor_field_unknown`, `page_rule_not_found`, `page_rule_out_of_window`, `page_rule_unknown_visitor`, `identity_unavailable`, `recent_visitor_no_match`, `recent_visitor_ambiguous`.\n\nBoolean decisions: `confidence_is_calibrated_probability` asks whether configuration alone establishes calibration; `ambiguous_graph_candidates_are_recent_evidence` asks whether unresolved/ambiguous graph candidates can be used as established recent identity edges; `caller_authentication_required` asks whether callers must authenticate/verify webhook delivery before this pure function; `atomic_receipt_effects_outbox_required` asks whether durable receipt, visitor/identify effects and outbox must share a transaction; `qualified_unique_constraints_required` asks whether durable scoped uniqueness must be enforced; `bounded_race_retry_after_rollback` asks whether a uniqueness race requires rollback/reload with bounded retry rather than committing partial effects; `replay_enqueues_duplicate_effects` asks whether a replay should enqueue effects again; `business_event_id_separate_from_transport` asks whether stable business IDs are separate from receipt/retry IDs; `outbox_consumer_idempotency_required` asks whether consumers must deduplicate stable events; `pure_function_guarantees_exactly_once` asks whether this pure function alone provides exactly-once delivery; `new_visitor_infers_prior_behavior` asks whether fallback creation supplies historical behavior.\n"
}

